== Confidence in a Good Estate

XI. It is no sign that affections are right, or that they are wrong, that they make the persons who have them exceedingly confident that what they experience is divine, and that they are in a good state.

Some argue against persons that they are deluded if they claim to be assured of their good state and to be carried beyond all doubt of the favor of God. They suppose that no such thing as a full and absolute assurance of hope is to be expected in the church of God, except in some very extraordinary circumstances, as in the case of martyrdom. This is contrary to the doctrine of Protestants, which their most celebrated writers have maintained against the Papists, and contrary to the plainest evidence of Scripture. It is plain that it was a common thing for the saints of whom we have a history, or particular account, in Scripture to be assured. God revealed and testified his special favor in the plainest and most certain way to #emph[Noah, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Moses, Daniel] and others. #emph[Job] often speaks of his sincerity and uprightness with the greatest confidence and assurance imaginable, often calling God to witness to it. And he says plainly, “I know that my Redeemer lives … I will see Him for myself … and not as a stranger” (Job 19:25 and following). #emph[David,] throughout the book of Psalms, almost everywhere speaks of God as his God without any hesitation and in the most confident way, glorying in him as his portion and heritage, his rock and confidence, his shield, salvation and high tower, and the like. #emph[Hezekiah] appeals to God as one who knew that he had walked before him faithfully and with wholehearted devotion (2 Kgs 20:3). Jesus Christ, in his dying discourse with his eleven disciples in the fourteenth, fifteenth and sixteenth chapters of #emph[John] (which was as it were Christ’s last will and testament to his disciples and to his whole church), often declares his special and everlasting love to them in the plainest and most definite terms. He promises them a future share with him in his glory in the most absolute way. And he tells them at the same time that he does so to this end, that their joy might be #emph[full:] “I have told you#super[+] these things so that My joy may be in you#super[+] and your#super[+] joy may be full” (John 15:11). See also the conclusion of his whole discourse, chapter 16:33: “I have told you#super[+] these things so that in Me you#super[+] may have peace. In the world you#super[+] will have tribulation. But take courage; I have overcome the world!” Christ was not afraid of speaking too plainly and definitely to them. He did not wish to hold them in the least suspense. And he concluded this last discourse of his with a prayer in their presence, in which he speaks with certainty to his Father of those eleven disciples. He speaks of them as having all savingly known him and believed in him, and received and kept his word. He says that they were not of the world, that for their sakes he sanctified himself, and that his will was that they should be with him in his glory. And he tells his Father that he spoke these things in his prayer to this end, that his joy might be fulfilled in them (verse 13). By these things it is evident that it agrees with Christ’s designs, and with the ordering and arrangement Christ has purposely made of things in his church, that sufficient and abundant provision should be made for his saints to have full assurance of their future glory.

The apostle #emph[Paul,] throughout all his epistles, speaks in an assured strain, always speaking with certainty of his special relation to Christ, his Lord and Master and Redeemer, and of his share in the future reward and his expectation of it. It would be endless to take notice of all the places that might be listed. I will mention only three or four. Gal 2:20: “Christ lives in me. The life I live in the body, I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself up for me.” Phil 1:21: “For to me, to live is Christ, and to die is gain.” 2 Tim 1:12: “I know whom I have believed, and I am convinced that He is able to guard what I have entrusted to Him for that day.” 2 Tim 4:7–8: “I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith. From now on there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous Judge, will award to me on that day.”

And the nature of the covenant of grace, and God’s declared ends in the appointment and arrangement of things in that covenant, plainly show that it is God’s design to make ample provision for the saints to have an assured hope of eternal life while living here on earth. For all things in that covenant are so ordered and arranged that everything might be made sure on God’s part. #emph[The covenant is ordered and secured in every part.] The promises are most full, very often repeated, and set forth in various ways. There are many witnesses and many seals, and God has confirmed his promises with an oath. And God’s declared design in all this is that the heirs of the promises might have an undoubting hope and full joy in an assurance of their future glory: “So when God wanted to make the unchanging nature of His purpose very clear to the heirs of the promise, He guaranteed it with an oath. Thus by two unchangeable things in which it is impossible for God to lie, we who have fled to take hold of the hope set before us may have strong consolation” (Heb 6:17–18). But all this would be useless for any such purpose as the saints’ strong consolation and hope of obtaining future glory if, in ordinary cases, they could not come to know their share in those sure promises. For God’s promises and oaths, however sure they are, cannot give strong hope and comfort to any particular person any further than he can know that those promises are made to him. And the provision made in Jesus Christ for believers to be made perfect as regards the conscience, as is indicated in Heb 9:9, is made in vain if assurance of freedom from the guilt of sin cannot be attained.

It further appears that assurance is attainable not only in some very extraordinary cases, because #emph[all] Christians are told to give all diligence to make their calling and election sure, and are told how they may do it (2 Pet 1:5–8). And not knowing whether Christ is in them or not is spoken of as a thing very unbecoming in Christians, and as a sign of something very blameworthy in them: “Do you#super[+] not know for yourselves#super[+] that Jesus Christ is in you#super[+]—unless you#super[+] actually fail the test?” (2 Cor 13:5). And it is implied that it is a sign of very blameworthy negligence in Christians if they practice Christianity in such a way as to remain uncertain of the reward, in 1 Cor 9:26: “Therefore I do not run uncertainly.” And to add no more, it is plain that Christians’ knowing their share in the saving benefits of Christianity is a thing ordinarily attainable, because the apostles tell us by what means #emph[Christians] (and not only #emph[apostles] and #emph[martyrs]) used to know this. 1 Cor 2:12: “We have not received the spirit of the world, but the Spirit who is from God, that we may know what God has freely given us.” And 1 John 2:3: “By this we can be sure that we have come to know Him: if we keep His commandments.” And verse 5: “By this we know that we are in Him.” Chapter 3:14: “We know that we have passed from death to life, because we love our brothers.” Verse 19: “And by this we will know that we belong to the truth, and will assure our hearts in His presence.” Verse 24: “And by this we know that He remains in us: by the Spirit He has given us.” So also chapter 4:13, and chapter 5:2, and verse 19.

Therefore it must be very unreasonable to decide that persons are hypocrites and their affections wrong because they seem to be free of doubt about their own salvation, and the affections they have seem to banish all fears of hell.

On the other hand, it is no sufficient reason to decide that men are saints and their affections gracious because the affections they have are accompanied by an exceeding confidence that their state is good and their affections divine.#footnote[“O professing Christian, look carefully to your foundation: do not be arrogant, but fear. You have perhaps done and suffered many things in and for religion; you have excellent gifts and sweet comforts, a warm zeal for God, and high confidence of your integrity. All this may be right, for all that I, or perhaps you, know; but even so, it is possible it may be false also. You have at times judged yourselves and pronounced yourselves upright; but remember, your final sentence is not yet pronounced by your judge. And what if God should weigh you again in his more just balance and say, #emph[Mene, Tekel:] you have been weighed on the scales and found deficient? What a shamed man you will be under such a sentence! #emph[Quae splendent in conspectu hominis, sordent in conspectu judicis:] things that are highly esteemed among men are an abomination in the sight of God; he does not see as man sees. Your heart may be false, and you not know it; indeed, it may be false, and you strongly confident of its integrity.” #emph[Flavel’s] #emph[touchstone of sincerity,] ch. 2. Sect. 5. “Some hypocrites are a great deal more confident than many saints.” #emph[Stoddard’s] discourse on #emph[the way to know sincerity and hypocrisy,] p. 128.]. Nothing can be argued with certainty from their confidence, however great and strong it seems to be. We may see a man who boldly calls God his Father, and commonly speaks in prayer in the boldest, most familiar and possessive language: #emph[“My Father, my dear Redeemer, my sweet Savior, my beloved,”] and the like. It may be a common thing for him to use the most confident expressions before men about the goodness of his state, such as, “I know for certain that God is my Father. I know as surely as there is a God in heaven that he is my God. I know I will go to heaven as well as if I were there. I know that God is now manifesting himself to my soul and is now smiling on me.” And he may seem to have finished forever with any inquiry or examination into his state, as a thing sufficiently known and beyond doubt, and to despise all who so much as suggest that there is some reason to doubt or fear whether all is right. Such things are no signs at all that it is indeed as he is confident it is.#footnote[“Does the work of faith in some believers bear on its top branches the full ripe fruits of a blessed assurance? Look what strong confidence and high-built persuasions of a share in God have at times been found in unsanctified ones! Indeed, this false assurance may be so strong that they dare boldly venture to go to the judgment seat of God and defend it there. Does the Spirit of God fill the heart of the assured believer with joy unspeakable and full of glory, giving them through faith a foretaste of heaven itself in those firstfruits of it? How near to this comes what the apostle supposes may be found in apostates!” #emph[Flavel’s] #emph[husbandry spiritualized,] ch. 12.]. Such an overbearing, high-handed and violent sort of confidence as this, seen in many, so eager to declare itself with a most glaring show in the sight of men, does not have the face of a true Christian assurance. It savors more of the spirit of the Pharisees. They never doubted that they were saints, and the most eminent of saints. They were bold to go to God and come up near to him, and lift up their eyes and thank him for the great distinction he had made between them and other men. And when Christ suggested that they were blind and without grace, they despised the suggestion: “Some of the Pharisees who were with Him heard this, and they asked Him, ‘Are we blind too?’” (John 9:40). Suppose that with their confidence they had more of the spirit of the #emph[tax collector,] who, with a sense of his exceeding unworthiness, stood far off and did not dare even to lift up his eyes to heaven, but beat his breast and cried out against himself as a sinner. Then their confidence would look more like the confidence of one who humbly trusts and hopes in Christ and has no confidence in himself.

If we only consider what the hearts of natural men are, what principles rule them, what blindness and deceit, what self-flattery, self-exaltation and self-confidence reign there, we need not wonder at all that their high opinion of themselves and their confidence in their happy circumstances should be as high and strong as mountains and as violent as a storm. This is so once conscience is blinded and convictions killed with false, high affections, and the principles just mentioned are let loose, fed and prompted by false joys and comforts stirred up by some pleasing imaginations impressed by #emph[Satan,] disguising himself as an angel of light.

Once a hypocrite is established in a false hope in this way, he does not have the things that often occasion the doubting of true saints to make him call his hope into question. #emph[First,] he does not have that cautious spirit, that great sense of the vast importance of a sure foundation, and that dread of being deceived. The comforts of true saints increase their awakening and caution, and a lively sense of how great a thing it is to appear before an infinitely holy, just and all-knowing judge. But false comforts put an end to these things and dreadfully stupefy the mind. #emph[Secondly,] the hypocrite does not have the knowledge of his own blindness and the deceitfulness of his own heart, and the low opinion of his own understanding, that the true saint has. Those who are deluded with false manifestations and affections are always highly conceited of their light and understanding. #emph[Thirdly,] the devil does not assault the hope of the hypocrite as he does the hope of a true saint. The devil is a great enemy to a true Christian hope, not only because it tends greatly to the comfort of the one who has it, but also because it is a thing of a holy, heavenly nature, greatly tending to promote and cherish grace in the heart, and a great incentive to strictness and diligence in the Christian life. But he is no enemy to the hope of a hypocrite, which above all things establishes his hold on the one who has it. A hypocrite may keep his hope without opposition as long as he lives, the devil never disturbing it or attempting to disturb it. But there is perhaps no true Christian whose hope is not assaulted by him. #emph[Satan] assaulted Christ himself on this point, whether he was the Son of God or not. And the servant is not above his master, nor the disciple above his Lord. It is enough for the disciple who is most privileged in this world to be like his master. #emph[Fourthly,] the one who has a false hope does not have the sight of his own corruptions that the saint has. A true Christian has ten times as much to do with his heart and its corruptions as a hypocrite. The sins of his heart and practice appear to him in their blackness. They look dreadful, and it often seems a very mysterious thing that any grace can be consistent with such corruption, or should be in such a heart. But a false hope hides corruption and covers it all over, and the hypocrite looks clean and bright in his own eyes.

There are two sorts of hypocrites. One sort are deceived by their outward morality and external religion; many of these are professed #emph[Arminians] in the doctrine of justification. The other sort are those who are deceived by false manifestations and elevations. These often cry down works and men’s own righteousness and talk much of free grace, but at the same time they make a righteousness of their manifestations and of their humbling, and exalt themselves to heaven with them. Mr. #emph[Shepard,] in his exposition of the parable of the ten virgins, distinguishes these two kinds of hypocrites by the names of #emph[legal] and #emph[evangelical] hypocrites, and often speaks of the latter as the worst. And it is evident that the latter are commonly by far the most confident in their hope, and the hardest to bring off from it. I have hardly known one instance of such a person in my life who has been undeceived. The chief grounds of the confidence of many of them are the very same kind of impulses and supposed revelations (sometimes with texts of Scripture and sometimes without) that so many have lately had about future events. They call these impulses about their good state the witness of the Spirit, entirely misunderstanding the nature of the witness of the Spirit, as I will show later. With those who have had visions and impulses about other things, these have generally revealed such things as they desire and are fond of. And it is no wonder that persons who pay attention to such things have the same sort of visions or impressions about their own eternal salvation, revealing to them that their sins are forgiven, that their names are written in the book of life, that they are in high favor with God, #emph[and so on.] This is especially so when they earnestly seek, expect and wait for evidence of their election and salvation in this way, as the surest and most glorious evidence of it. Nor is it any wonder that when they have such a supposed revelation of their good state, it raises in them the highest degree of confidence in it. Abundant experience shows that those who are led away by impulses and imagined revelations are extremely confident. They suppose that the great Jehovah has declared these and those things to them, and since they have his immediate testimony, a strong confidence is the highest virtue. So they are bold to say, #emph[“I know this or that … I know for certain … I am as sure of it as that I exist,”] and the like, and they despise all argument and inquiry in the case. And above all else, it is easy to account for impressions and impulses about something so pleasing, so suited to their self-love and pride, as their being the dear children of God, distinguished in his favor from most in the world, making them strongly confident. This is especially so when, along with their impulses and revelations, they have high affections, which they take to be the most eminent exercises of grace. I have known of several persons who had a foolish desire for something of a temporal nature, through a violent passion that possessed them. They had been earnestly pursuing the thing they desired, and had met with great difficulty and many discouragements in it. But at last they had an impression or supposed revelation that they would obtain what they sought, and they looked on it as a sure promise from the Most High. This made them most ridiculously confident, against every kind of reason to convince them otherwise, and against all events working against them. And nothing prevents persons who are seeking their salvation from being deceived by similar delusive impressions, and being made confident of that in the same way.

The confidence of many of this sort of hypocrites, whom Mr. #emph[Shepard] calls #emph[evangelical hypocrites,] is like the confidence of some madmen who think they are kings: they will maintain it against every kind of reason and evidence. And in one sense it is much more immovable than a truly gracious assurance. A true assurance is upheld only by the soul’s being kept in a holy frame, and grace being maintained in lively exercise. If the actings of grace decline greatly in the Christian, and he falls into a lifeless frame, he loses his assurance. But this kind of confidence of hypocrites will not be shaken by sin. They (some of them at least) will keep up their boldness in their hope in the most corrupt frames and wicked ways, which is a sure evidence of their delusion.#footnote[Mr. #emph[Shepard] speaks of it as a #emph[presumptuous peace that is not interrupted and broken by evil works.] And he says that the #emph[Spirit will sigh, and not sing, in that bosom from which corrupt dispositions and passions break out;] and that #emph[though men in such frames may seem to maintain the consolation of the Spirit, and not suspect their hypocrisy, under the pretense of trusting the Lord’s mercy, yet they cannot escape the condemnation of the word.] Parable of the ten virgins, Part 1. P. 139. Dr. #emph[Ames] speaks of it as a thing by which the peace of a wicked man may be distinguished from the peace of a godly man, that the peace of a wicked man continues whether he performs the duties of piety and righteousness or not, provided he avoids those crimes that appear horrid to nature itself. Cases of conscience, Lib. III. Chapter 7.].

And here I cannot help observing that there are certain doctrines often preached to the people which need to be delivered with more caution and explanation than they often are. For as many understand them, they tend greatly to establish this delusion and false confidence of hypocrites. The doctrines I speak of are those of #emph[Christians living by faith, not by sight; their giving glory to God by trusting him in the dark; living on Christ, and not on experiences; not making their good frames the foundation of their faith.] These are indeed excellent and important doctrines when rightly understood, but corrupt and destructive as many understand them. The Scripture speaks of living or walking by faith and not by sight in no other ways than these, #emph[namely:] being governed by a regard to eternal things, which are the objects of faith and are not seen, and not by a regard to temporal things, which are seen; believing things revealed which we never saw with bodily eyes; and also living by faith in the promise of future things, without yet seeing or enjoying the things promised, or knowing how they can be fulfilled. This will be easily evident to anyone who looks over the Scriptures that speak of #emph[faith] in opposition to #emph[sight,] such as 2 Cor 4:18; 5:7; Heb 11:1, 8, 13, 17, 27, 29; Rom 8:24; John 20:29. But this doctrine, as many understand it, is that Christians ought to believe and trust in Christ firmly without spiritual sight or light, even though they are in a dark, dead frame and for the present have no spiritual experiences or manifestations. And it is truly the duty of those who are in darkness in this way to come out of darkness into light, and believe. But that they should confidently believe and trust while they still remain without spiritual light or sight is a doctrine contrary to Scripture, and absurd. The Scripture knows nothing of any such faith in Christ, of the working of God, that is not founded on a spiritual sight of Christ. The believing in Christ which goes with a title to everlasting life is a #emph[seeing of the Son and believing in him] (John 6:40). True faith in Christ is never exercised any further than persons #emph[behold as in a mirror the glory of the Lord,] and have #emph[the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ] (2 Cor 3:18; 4:6). Those into whose minds #emph[the light of the glorious gospel of Christ, who is the image of God, does not shine, do not believe] (2 Cor 4:4). Faith that is without spiritual light is not the faith of the children of the light and of the day, but the presumption of the children of darkness. And therefore to press and urge them to believe without any spiritual light or sight tends greatly to further the delusions of the prince of darkness. Not only can men not exercise faith without some spiritual light, but they can exercise faith only in exact proportion to the spiritual light they have. Men will trust in God no further than they know him. And they cannot be in the exercise of faith in him one bit further than they have a sight of his fullness and faithfulness #emph[in exercise.] Nor can they have the exercise of trust in God any further than they are in a #emph[gracious frame.] Those who are in a dead, carnal frame doubtless ought to trust in God, because that would be the same thing as coming out of their bad frame and turning to God. But to exhort men to trust in God confidently, and so to keep up their hope and peace, though they are not in a gracious frame and still remain so, is in effect the same thing as exhorting them to trust in God confidently, but not with a gracious trust. And what is that but wicked presumption? It is just as impossible for men to have a strong or lively trust in God when they have no lively exercises of grace, or felt Christian experiences, as it is for them to be in the lively exercises of grace without the exercises of grace.

It is true that it is the duty of God’s people to trust in him when they are in darkness, and though they remain in darkness, in this sense: they ought to trust in God when the face of his providence is dark and looks as though God had forsaken them and did not hear their prayers; when many clouds gather and many enemies surround them with a fearsome look, threatening to swallow them up; when all the events of providence seem to be against them, and all circumstances seem to make the promises of God difficult to fulfill; and when God must be trusted out of sight, #emph[that is,] when we cannot see how it is possible for him to fulfill his word, and everything but God’s mere word makes it look unlikely, so that if persons believe, they must hope against hope. In this way the ancient #emph[patriarchs,] and #emph[Job,] and the psalmist, and #emph[Jeremiah, Daniel, Shadrach, Meshach] and #emph[Abednego,] and the apostle #emph[Paul] gave glory to God by trusting in God in darkness. And we have many instances of such a glorious, victorious faith in the eleventh chapter of #emph[Hebrews.] But how different a thing this is from trusting in God without spiritual sight while being at the same time in a dead and carnal frame!

There is also such a thing as spiritual light being let into the soul in one way when it is not in another. And so there is such a thing as the saints trusting in God, and also knowing their good state, when they lack some kinds of experience. For instance, they may have clear views of God’s sufficiency and faithfulness, and so trust in him confidently and know that they are his children, and at the same time not have such clear and sweet ideas of his love as at other times. For it was so with Christ himself in his last suffering. And they may have views of much of God’s sovereignty, holiness and all-sufficiency, enabling them to submit to him quietly and exercise a sweet and most encouraging hope in God’s fullness, when they are not satisfied of their own good state. But how different these things are from trusting in God confidently without spiritual light or experience!

Those who insist in this way on persons living by faith when they have no experience and are in very bad frames are also very absurd in their notions of faith. What they mean by faith is believing that they are in a good state. So they count it a dreadful sin for persons to doubt their state, whatever frames they are in and whatever wicked things they do, because, they say, it is the great and heinous sin of unbelief. And in their view the best man, who puts the most honor on God, is the one who keeps his hope of his good state most confidently and immovably when he has the least light or experience, that is to say, when he is in the worst and most wicked frame and way. For that, if you please, is a sign that he is strong in faith, giving glory to God, and against hope believes in hope. But from what Bible do they learn this notion of faith, that it is a man’s confidently believing that he is in a good state?#footnote[“Men do not know that they are godly by believing that they are godly. We know many things by faith: ‘By faith we understand that the universe was formed at God’s command’ (Heb 11:3). Faith is the evidence of things not seen (Heb 11:1). This is how men know the Trinity of persons of the Godhead; that Jesus Christ is the Son of God; that he who believes in him will have eternal life; the resurrection of the dead. And if God should tell a saint that he has grace, he might know it by believing the word of God. But it is not in this way that godly men know that they have grace. It is not revealed in the word, and the Spirit of God does not testify it to particular persons.” #emph[Stoddard’s] nature of saving conversion, p. 83, 84.] If this is faith, the Pharisees had faith in an eminent degree, and some of them, Christ teaches, committed the unpardonable sin against the Holy Spirit. The Scripture represents faith as that by which men are #emph[brought into] a good state, and therefore it cannot be the same thing as believing that they #emph[are already] in a good state. To suppose that faith consists in persons believing that they are in a good state is in effect the same as supposing that faith consists in a person’s believing that he has faith, or believing that he believes.

Indeed, persons’ doubting their good state may in several respects #emph[arise from] unbelief. It may be from unbelief, or because they have so little faith, that they have so little #emph[evidence] of their good state. If they had more experience of the actings of faith, and so more experience of the exercise of grace, they would have clearer evidence that their state was good, and so their doubts would be removed. And then their doubting of their state may be from unbelief in this way: there may be many things that are good evidences of a work of grace in them, and yet they doubt very much whether they are really in a state of favor with God, because it is #emph[they,] people so unworthy, who have done so much to provoke God to anger against them. Their doubts in such a case arise from unbelief, in that they arise from a lack of a sufficient sense #emph[of,] and reliance #emph[on,] the infinite riches of God’s grace and the sufficiency of Christ for the chief of sinners. They may also be from unbelief when they doubt their state because of the mystery of God’s dealings with them, when they are not able to reconcile such dispensations with God’s favor to them. Or they may be from unbelief when they doubt whether they have any share in the promises because, from the face of providence, the promises appear so unlikely to be fulfilled, and the difficulties in the way are so many and great. Such doubting arises from a lack of dependence on God’s almighty power and on his knowledge and wisdom, as infinitely above theirs. But even so, in such persons, their unbelief and their doubting of their state are not the same thing, though the one arises from the other.

Persons may be greatly to blame for doubting their state on grounds such as these last mentioned. And they may be to blame that they have no more grace, and no more of its present exercises and experiences to be an evidence to them of the goodness of their state. Men are doubtless to blame for being in a dead, carnal frame. But when they are in such a frame and have no felt experience of the exercises of grace, but on the contrary are very much under the power of their lusts and an unchristian spirit, they are not to blame for doubting their state. In the nature of things, it is as impossible for a holy and Christian hope to be kept alive in its clearness and strength in such circumstances as it is to keep the light in the room when the candle is put out, or to keep the bright sunshine in the air when the sun has gone down. Distant experiences, when darkened by present prevailing lust and corruption, will never keep a gracious confidence and assurance alive. Rather, it sickens and declines under this as surely as a little child under repeated blows on the head with a hammer. Nor is it at all to be lamented that persons doubt their state in such circumstances. On the contrary, it is desirable and in every way best that they should. It agrees with the wise and merciful arrangement of things that God has established that it should be so. For God has so contrived and arranged things in his dispensations toward his own people that when their #emph[love] declines, and its exercises fail or become weak, #emph[fear] should arise. For then they need it, to restrain them from sin and to stir them to care for the good of their souls, and so to rouse them to watchfulness and diligence in religion. But God has so ordered it that when love rises and is in vigorous exercise, fear should vanish and be driven away. For then they do not need it, having a higher and more excellent principle in exercise to restrain them from sin and stir them up to their duty. There are no other principles under whose influence human nature lies that will ever make men conscientious but one of these two, #emph[fear] or #emph[love.] And therefore, if one of these did not prevail as the other declined, God’s people, when fallen into dead and carnal frames, when love is asleep, would indeed be lamentably exposed. And therefore God has wisely ordained that these two opposite principles of love and fear should rise and fall like the two opposite scales of a balance: when one rises, the other sinks. Light and darkness necessarily and unavoidably follow each other. If light prevails, darkness ceases just so much and no more, and if light declines, darkness prevails just so much. So it is in the heart of a child of God. If divine love declines and falls asleep, and lust prevails, the light and joy of hope go out, and dark fear and doubting arise. And if on the contrary divine love prevails and comes into lively exercise, this brings in the brightness of hope and drives away black lust, and fear with it. Love is the #emph[Spirit of adoption,] or the childlike principle. If that slumbers, men fall under fear, which is the #emph[spirit of bondage,] or the slavish principle, and so on the contrary. And if love, or the spirit of adoption, is carried to a great height, it drives away all fear completely and gives full assurance, in agreement with the apostle’s words in 1 John 4:18: “There is no fear in love, but perfect love drives out fear.” These two opposite principles of lust and holy love bring hope and fear into the hearts of God’s children in proportion as they prevail, that is, when left to their own natural influence, without something coming in from outside or by accident: such as the disease of melancholy, ignorance of doctrine, prejudices of upbringing, wrong instruction, false principles, particular temptations, #emph[and so on.]

The Spirit of God casts out fear in no other way than by the prevailing of love. Nor does his Spirit ever keep fear out when love is asleep. At such a time all the saint’s self-examinations, and poring over past experience in order to establish his peace and get assurance, are in vain. For it is contrary to the nature of things, as God has arranged them, that he should have assurance at such a time.

Those, therefore, who exhort others to be confident in their hope when in dead frames directly thwart God’s wise and gracious arrangement of things. They do it under the notion of #emph[living by faith] and #emph[not by sight, and trusting God in the dark,] and #emph[living on Christ, and not on experiences;] and they warn them not to doubt their good state, lest they be guilty of the dreadful sin of unbelief. And this has a direct tendency to establish the most presumptuous hypocrites, and to keep them from ever calling their state into question, however much wickedness rages and reigns in their hearts and prevails in their lives, under the notion of honoring God by #emph[hoping against hope] and trusting in God confidently when things look very dark. And doubtless vast harm has been done in this way.

Persons cannot be said to forsake Christ and live on their experiences of the exercises of grace merely because they take them and use them as evidences of grace. For there are no other evidences they can or ought to take. But persons may be said to live on their experiences when they make a righteousness of them. Instead of keeping their eye on God’s glory and Christ’s excellence, they turn their eyes away from these objects outside them onto themselves, to entertain their minds by viewing their own attainments and high experiences and the great things they have met with. They are bright and beautiful in their own eyes, and rich and grown wealthy in their own estimation, and they think God has as admiring an esteem of them on the same account as they have of themselves. This is living on experiences and not on Christ, and it is more abominable in the sight of God than the gross immoralities of those who make no claims to religion. But this is a far different thing from merely making use of experiences as evidences of a share in a glorious Redeemer.

But to return from this digression, I would mention one thing more under the general head I am on.
